alf, second half), you try removing each chunk. That matches typical reduction.\n\nSo let's interpret granularity as the number of chunks to split the list into (i.e., number of partitions). Start with 2 partitions. For each chunk (partition), try removing it. If removal succeeds (required items still present), accept removal, restart with slightly coarser granularity (meaning fewer partitions? Coarser = larger chunks = fewer partitions). So increase the number of partitions? No, coarser means larger parts, so fewer parts. So \"slightly coarser\" could mean decrease the number of partitions by 1? But \"double granularity when no removal succeeds\" would then double the number of partitions, making finer. That would be: success -> coarser (fewer partitions), failure -> finer (more partitions). That is the opposite of typical dd but matches the description: start with granularity 2 (2 partitions). If a removal succeeds, we go to slightly coarser (1 partition? That would be just the whole list, which can't be removed because it would remove required items). But \"slightly coarser\" could mean increase partition size, i.e., reduce number of partitions by 1, so from 2 to 1. But then that's the whole list, which cannot be removed. Then we would double granularity? That would go to 2 again? Not good.\n\nMaybe \"granularity\" is the size of the window (step) for non-overlapping chunks. For example, with granularity 2, we take chunks of size 2 starting from index 0: [0,1], [2,3], [4,5], ... Then try removing each in order. If a removal succeeds, we restart with slightly coarser granularity, meaning increase chunk size by 1 (e.g., from 2 to 3). \"Double granularity when no removal succeeds\" would then double chunk size (2 -> 4, 4 -> 8). And \"stop at singleton granularity\" means stop when chunk size becomes 1 (i.e., we have tried single items). That makes sense: start with size 2, after success increase by 1, after failure double. Eventually we will try size 1 when we doubl