e value and get 1? That would only happen if we start from 0.5? No.\n\nAlternatively, \"stop at singleton granularity\" might mean we stop when the chunk size equals the total length (singleton chunk covering whole list)? That is unlikely.\n\nPerhaps \"granularity\" refers to the number of chunks (i.e., we partition the list into that many equal parts). Start with 2 parts. Try each part. If removal accepted, restart with slightly fewer parts (coarser), e.g., 1 part? But 1 part is the whole list. If no removal accepted, double the number of parts (finer) to 4, 8, ... until the part size becomes 1 (i.e., number of parts equals length). Then stop. That makes sense: \"singleton granularity\" means each chunk is a single element, i.e., number of parts = length. And \"double granularity\" means double the number of parts. \"Slightly coarser\" after success: since we succeeded, we want to go to coarser (larger chunks, fewer parts). So we reduce the number of parts by a small amount, maybe by 1? Or go to half? But \"slightly coarser\" suggests a small adjustment. If we have N parts and we succeed, we might try N-1 parts? But that would be slightly coarser. If we fail, we double N (fine). This is a plausible algorithm: start with N=2 partitions. For each partition (left to right), try removing it. If removal succeeds, reduce N by 1 (coarser) and start over with the new list. If no removal for any partition, double N (but not exceeding the length of the current list). Stop when N equals the length of the list (each partition is a single item). This matches typical delta debugging where you start with 2 subsets and refine to finer granularities only when necessary.\n\nLet's test this with case 0 (items length 10, required t0,t9).\n\nStart N=2. Partition list into 2 parts: part0 = first 5 items [t0..t4], part1 = last 5 [t5..t9]. (Assuming equal size, remainder handled later.) Try remove part0: would remove t0, so fails. Try remove part1: would remove t9, fails. So no removal. T