y part0: remove t0,t1 -> removes t1, fails. part1: remove t3 -> leaves t0,t1,t4 -> t1,t4 present, accept. Remove, list [t0,t1,t4]. Restart N=2. Parts: [t0,t1] and [t4]. Try part0: remove t0,t1 -> removes t1, fails. part1: remove t4 -> removes t4, fails. No removal. Double N to 4, cap at 3 (length). N=3, parts: each single: [t0],[t1],[t4]. Try part0: remove t0 -> leaves t1,t4 -> accept. Remove, list [t1,t4]. Restart N=2. Parts: [t1] and [t4]. Removal fails. N=2 == length, stop. Final [t1,t4].\n\nCase 2: required [t1,t6]. Similar process should yield [t1,t6] eventually? Possibly.\n\nThus the algorithm yields a minimal list containing exactly the required items (assuming they are distinct). The reduced_items are the list after reduction. attempts count the number of times we tried to remove a chunk (each partition check). one_minimal should be true if no single item can be removed (i.e., at the end we check each single item removal). In our process, we ended with list where each required item is present, and removing any single item would remove a required one, so one_minimal = True.\n\nThis interpretation seems plausible and yields deterministic results. The algorithm:\n\n- Given items list L, required set R.\n- Define function can_remove(chunk_indices): returns True if after removing the items at those indices, the set of required items is still subset of remaining items.\n- Granularity parameter N (number of partitions). Start N=2.\n- While True:\n    - Partition L into N contiguous chunks as evenly as possible.\n    - For each chunk in left-to-right order:\n        - attempts += 1\n        - If can_remove(items in that chunk):\n            - Remove that chunk from L.\n            - Set N = N - 1 (if N>1 else N=2?) \"slightly coarser\" -> reduce N by 1, but ensure N >= 1? If N becomes 0 or 1, we might set to 1? Actually if N=2 and success, N becomes 1, then we'll have only one chunk (the whole list). That chunk removal may fail. Then double N to 2. So N can be 1.\n 